    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>'s army was larger, said to number up to 110,000 men, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>'s small cavalry raids on his supply depots at Gushi and then Wuchao decided the campaign.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> ignored the advice of Ju Shou, Tian Feng and Xu You at several points, and Xu You then defected and told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> where Wuchao was weakly guarded.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> died in June 202 and his sons fought each other, so that by 207 most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In his proclamation of victory to Emperor Xian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> claimed to have killed 70,000 enemy troops.</p></aside>
    </section>

